{:db/id 17592186175970 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186175820 :question/id #uuid "616e03ca-b0fc-53f1-baf0-8034d7731891" :question/math-academy-id "e-8885" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z) = \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 4\\sin{t}, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4}+\\dfrac{y^2}{16}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\langle x,y \\rangle = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 4\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\frac{16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}2\\cos{t}}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d646eda-468b-5ae5-b7b0-0d53a948a808" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049672 :topic/math-academy-id 2746 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Ellipses"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186176191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82401ace-c226-55ca-a5ef-b2e14e6c7e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh t" :db/id 17592186173405} {:answer/id #uuid "ba92dba1-602b-58ac-b8f4-1c295e03bc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z^{2}" :db/id 17592186173406} {:answer/id #uuid "bd40b8af-bb6c-5853-82d3-8cfcb0cb65fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos t" :db/id 17592186173407} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d2715b-fe19-5704-96cd-0396e7d21c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sinh t" :db/id 17592186173408} {:answer/id #uuid "1624dc22-76b6-5d4f-8414-753e14dc0f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin t" :db/id 17592186173409}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f7497f-0839-50b7-addb-6102a75d75ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186173404}] :question/id #uuid "700c7d88-ca90-5a51-8c35-1157cc4b950b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103980" :question/problem "$f(t, z) = ⟨2\\cos t, _____, z⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t, z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$. What is the second component of $f$?"} {:db/id 17592186176192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92c9230f-2b9f-51e3-9538-10bd091526c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}" :db/id 17592186173411} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a35d31-72bf-5f4e-b5a6-6a58107f6699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin t" :db/id 17592186173412} {:answer/id #uuid "1684003c-ff41-563f-a3cf-65d230f52f9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\tan t" :db/id 17592186173413} {:answer/id #uuid "48a1f2d5-f114-50f5-87b9-26b1a552c875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin t" :db/id 17592186173414} {:answer/id #uuid "70d8186f-639c-5309-a214-783401a741ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id 17592186173415}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f84bd25-bf3d-5636-bb7b-be37465bcd0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186173410}] :question/id #uuid "2389989c-4630-57be-9153-1917329cb33a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103981" :question/problem "$f(t, y) = ⟨2\\cos t, y, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t, y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9x^{2} + 4z^{2} = 36$. What is the third component of $f$?"} {:db/id 17592186362486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8475316c-8579-5579-ba08-41d5bb64d787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id 17592186362488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8475316c-8579-5579-ba08-41d5bb64d787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id 17592186362488} :answer-field/id #uuid "795f305e-67f0-5314-a428-976bafef3452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186362487}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ad7c087-31de-5f2c-99f2-5a1c344721d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167887" :question/problem "A parametrization of the cylindrical heater is $f(\\theta ,z)=⟨$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the circle\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1\n$$\n\nin the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9}+\\frac{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(\\theta ,z)\\in C$ and $C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$"} {:db/id 17592186365426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fce6433b-566b-5373-9f60-54afb5810670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365428} {:answer/id #uuid "878c8cef-35cb-51a6-b38b-2b4f75ac5495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365429} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0a4702-a64f-517a-b849-f468974a48c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5z" :db/id 17592186365430} {:answer/id #uuid "13a1e5a6-e5dd-557e-99e3-9aff0e9c76d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186365431} {:answer/id #uuid "aade5251-2de5-58c4-8f34-8bc109e8b273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878c8cef-35cb-51a6-b38b-2b4f75ac5495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e118899-8a0e-5b48-82c5-20aecfe54e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186365427}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9673b35d-7ab7-5053-8bde-0eee9a8197b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164526" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} {:db/id 17592186365433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57deffd8-e545-55f1-abe6-d4f07da38672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365435} {:answer/id #uuid "97214db6-700f-5149-8a8a-bfcdaa78370c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id 17592186365436} {:answer/id #uuid "6053ecc2-43ad-511c-a23d-90c7de3c0861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365437} {:answer/id #uuid "63bb069f-8fe9-576c-b784-794cce9e2f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365438} {:answer/id #uuid "834d7cec-cbed-5efe-bd29-15484716878c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "834d7cec-cbed-5efe-bd29-15484716878c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365439} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7340ee-5e79-5612-8e06-781561cbe7a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186365434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c053e26-9cc8-557f-ade7-75bec2ed7dea" :question/math-academy-id "q-164530" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},y,\\,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} {:db/id 17592186365440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e83a2b2d-5277-5f92-aa24-babf0be055ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365442} {:answer/id #uuid "56c5385c-0ed1-5c6e-a60b-b0dbdd5b7d6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{y}^{2}" :db/id 17592186365443} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfd544b-056e-57ec-8a1c-f726d0f6a322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365444} {:answer/id #uuid "37afd173-73ed-540e-9cb3-0cdb79d64880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365445} {:answer/id #uuid "187f9c55-a141-5a67-9004-71a8fa64ec6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y" :db/id 17592186365446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37afd173-73ed-540e-9cb3-0cdb79d64880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186365445} :answer-field/id #uuid "013561ed-7b9a-55a6-b47c-a86adecda9e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186365441}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12c956ca-b0c1-5c13-bc7f-2c6e08417c3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164534" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨\\text{_____},y,\\,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4{x}^{2}+25{z}^{2}=100.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+25{z}^{2}=100\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$\n\nin $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} {:db/id 17592186389539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707fbf1b-bf7d-5995-b7cc-be64436a3be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389541} {:answer/id #uuid "d22e14ca-11b5-5af0-a855-e0dc3f5271d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389542} {:answer/id #uuid "1adc692e-fe37-5019-893d-bd451359ba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389543} {:answer/id #uuid "c552ac7b-ee72-589e-9065-cf0a29c3f734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389544} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ee24c-0ae3-5eec-973f-6e3492e4e1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186389545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22e14ca-11b5-5af0-a855-e0dc3f5271d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bea9a704-6834-5c1c-808b-11ae8ac08e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186389540}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aefa1f59-5dce-5a67-8312-bce584cb45c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164522" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{36} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} {:db/id 17592186389553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed22918-85fb-5c79-9620-31cecfbbc27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{z}^{2}" :db/id 17592186389555} {:answer/id #uuid "d4533c8f-a193-52ed-a9d9-fc9ccfbdbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389556} {:answer/id #uuid "974a57ae-291f-5842-95b3-64380db1d779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389557} {:answer/id #uuid "814a367c-72f0-5937-973d-1f16f3d1e3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389558} {:answer/id #uuid "487b991c-c37d-5069-8337-bf2040028617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4533c8f-a193-52ed-a9d9-fc9ccfbdbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id 17592186389556} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d86410-beeb-5cce-9a2f-324bd43cac89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186389554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353926d7-638c-5e50-a022-4791f5ce8a3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164524" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{cos}^{2}⁡t}{25}+\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡t}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"}
